{"name":"Mussel White","hex":"#f0e2de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mussel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several groups of bivalve shellfish with elongated, asymmetrical shells.\nA saltwater mussel, usually edible, of the order Mytilida in subclass Pteriomorphia.\nA freshwater mussel, usually edible, of the order Unionida in subclass Palaeoheterodonta.\nAny of certain other bivalves of somewhat similar appearance, such as the zebra mussel and quagga mussel of the family Dreissenidae in subclass Heterodonta.","A saltwater mussel, usually edible, of the order Mytilida in subclass Pteriomorphia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mussel"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mussel","blocks":[{"text":"Mussel is the common name used for members of several families of bivalve molluscs, from saltwater and freshwater habitats. These groups have in common a shell whose outline is elongated and asymmetrical compared with other edible clams, which are often more or less rounded or oval."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/CornishMussels.JPG/330px-CornishMussels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mussel","wikidata":"Q13042834"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Miesmuscheln-2.jpg/500px-Miesmuscheln-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Miesmuscheln-2.jpg/1280px-Miesmuscheln-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Miesmuscheln-2.jpg/500px-Miesmuscheln-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Marine blue mussel, Mytilus edulis, showing some of the inner anatomy. The white posterior adductor muscle is visible in the upper image, and has been cut in the lower image to allow the valves to open fully.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiesmuscheln-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Mytilus_with_byssus.jpg/500px-Mytilus_with_byssus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Mytilus_with_byssus.jpg/500px-Mytilus_with_byssus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A Mytilus with its byssus clearly showing, at Ocean Beach, San Francisco, California","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMytilus_with_byssus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Starfishmussel.jpg/500px-Starfishmussel.jpg?